icle", "slug": "hi-nrg"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q606393", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hi-NRG"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Hi-NRG significantly shaped the electronic dance music landscape and promoted LGBTQ+ inclusion in mainstream culture, influencing countless artists and genres in the decades that followed.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T13:12:39Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30545273640", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Genre of uptempo disco or electronic dance music Created c. 1978.", "newValue": "Hi-NRG is a genre of uptempo disco and electronic dance music that emerged in the late 1970s, primarily characterized by its fast beats, synthesizer riffs, and an energetic, often euphoric sound. The term "Hi-NRG" first gained prominence in 1982, but its roots trace back to the disco scene of New York City during the late 1970s when artists like Patrick Hernandez and the Village People began to incorporate electronic elements into their music. This genre is significant not only for its contribution to the evolution of dance music but also for its role in the LGBTQ+ community, where it became a staple in clubs and parties, symbolizing freedom and expression at a time when social attitudes toward sexuality were changing dramatically.

The development of Hi-NRG was fueled by technological advancements in music production, particularly the introduction of synthesizers and drum machines. Notable tracks such as "I Feel for You" by Prince and "You Make Me Feel (Mighty Real)" by Sylvester exemplify the genre's hallmark sound. By the mid-1980s, Hi-NRG had influenced the burgeoning house music scene and was pivotal in the evolution of electronic dance music (EDM). Its pulsating rhythms and infectious melodies laid the groundwork for future genres, including Eurodance and techno, helping to shape the modern club experience.

